  "answer_text": "Whether Crimea belongs to Russia depends on the distinction between international law (*de jure* status) and the physical reality on the ground (*de facto* control). \n\n### 1. International Law and Recognition (*De Jure*)\nAccording to international law and the vast majority of the global community, **Crimea belongs to Ukraine**. \n\n* **The Annexation:** In March 2014, following the Ukrainian revolution, Russian forces seized control of the Crimean Peninsula and organized a referendum on joining Russia.\n* **International Consensus:** The United Nations General Assembly, along with the United States, the European Union, and most sovereign nations, rejected the referendum as invalid because it was conducted under military occupation and violated the Ukrainian constitution.\n* **Legal Status:** International bodies and treaties (such as the 1994 Budapest Memorandum) recognize Crimea as Ukrainian territory. The international community generally refers to Crimea as \"temporarily occupied Ukrainian territory\".\n\n### 2. Physical Reality and Administration (*De Facto*)\nIn practice, **Russia exercises administrative and military control over Crimea**.\n\n* **Russian Government:** Since the 2014 annexation, Russia has integrated the peninsula into its political, economic, and legal systems. Russia administers the territory as two federal subjects: the Republic of Crimea and the federal city of Sevastopol.\n* **Daily Life:** The currency, tax system, laws, education system, and time zone in Crimea are all operational under Russian authority. \n\n### 3. The Ongoing Conflict\nCrimea remains a primary flashpoint in the ongoing Russia-Ukraine war. \n* **Ukraine's Position:** The Ukrainian government has repeatedly stated that its ultimate victory in the war requires the liberation of all occupied lands, including Crimea, to restore its internationally recognized 1991 borders. Ukraine regularly targets Russian military bases, supply lines, and naval vessels in and around the peninsula.\n* **Russia's Position:** The Kremlin maintains that Crimea's status is non-negotiable and considers it an inseparable part of the Russian Federation.",
